window.DEEP={"groups":{"british":{"label":"The British Isles","dnaPct":48,"cards":[{"era":"Upper Palaeolithic Europe (shared deep ancestry)","start":-45000,"end":-15000,"title":"The Common Source: Ice-Age Europe","blurb":"Trace every British layer back far enough and the lines converge on the early modern humans who settled Ice-Age Europe after the dispersal out of Africa. These Upper Palaeolithic people carried a small percentage of Neanderthal DNA from earlier interbreeding, sheltered through cold phases in southern refugia, and produced the cave art, figurines and bone tools of the Aurignacian, Gravettian and Magdalenian. From this ancestral pool later branched the lineages \u2014 hunter-gatherers, Anatolian farmers and steppe herders \u2014 whose successive waves shaped British DNA. This is the deepest shared root, where the British Isles story merges into the common human past.","sites":"Goyet & Doln\u00ed V\u011bstonice (early Europeans), Iberian/Italian/Balkan refugia, Aurignacian-to-Magdalenian cave-art sites","genetics":"Upper Palaeolithic Europeans; ~2% Neanderthal admixture; deep source pool from which WHG, ANF and steppe-related lineages later diverged","certainty":"deep-inference"},{"era":"European Western Hunter-Gatherers / Villabruna ancestry","start":-15000,"end":-9700,"title":"Out of the Ice: The Villabruna Ancestry","blurb":"Before Britain was an island, its foragers belonged to a wider post-glacial population that spread across Europe as the ice retreated. Genetically these carry 'Villabruna-related' ancestry \u2014 the source of the Western Hunter-Gatherer component later seen in Cheddar Man. Drawing on Magdalenian reindeer-hunters and other late-glacial groups that had sheltered in southern refugia, they followed game north into a thawing tundra-then-forest Europe. They made fine microliths and were highly mobile. This is the deep European hunter-gatherer foundation onto which Britain's later layers were built.","sites":"Villabruna (Italy), Magdalenian cave-art sites, Gough's Cave (late-glacial reoccupation)","genetics":"Villabruna-related WHG ancestry, ancestral to later NW-European hunter-gatherers; associated with the dark-skin/light-eye profile","certainty":"well-supported"},{"era":"British Mesolithic Hunter-Gatherers","start":-9700,"end":-4000,"title":"Cheddar Man and the First Britons After the Ice","blurb":"After the last glaciation, hunter-gatherers recolonized Britain when it was still joined to Europe by the now-drowned plain of Doggerland. At Star Carr in Yorkshire they left antler frontlet headdresses, a timber platform and worked wood beside a vanished lake; in the Mendip caves they buried their dead, including the ~10,000-year-old Cheddar Man. His genome suggested these Western Hunter-Gatherers probably had dark to brown skin together with blue or pale eyes \u2014 though the researchers stressed the skin-colour prediction is probabilistic, not certain. They fished and hunted red deer and aurochs. Their ancestry was largely overwritten by incoming farmers, surviving only as a small fraction in modern Britons.","sites":"Star Carr, Gough's Cave (Cheddar Man), Aveline's Hole, submerged Doggerland finds","genetics":"Western Hunter-Gatherer (WHG); probable dark-skin + light-eye combination (probabilistic prediction); Y-haplogroup likely I2; largely replaced by Neolithic farmers","certainty":"well-supported"},{"era":"Pre-Pottery Neolithic Anatolia (G\u00f6bekli Tepe & Karahan Tepe)","start":-9500,"end":-8000,"title":"Monuments Before the Harvest","blurb":"In the hills of southeastern Anatolia, just as farming was beginning, communities raised some of the world's oldest known monumental architecture: G\u00f6bekli Tepe and related sites of the Ta\u015f Tepeler ('Stone Hills') region, including Karahan Tepe and Saybur\u00e7. They carved T-shaped limestone pillars bearing arms, foxes, snakes and vultures, and at Karahan Tepe cut a chamber lined with pillars overlooked by a carved human head. Recent excavation has tempered the old 'temple-only, no settlement' view: cisterns, grinding equipment and domestic structures show people lived and processed food at these sites, not merely gathered to worship. Striking finds continue, including a near life-size human statue at Karahan Tepe. These are monuments built by foragers on the cusp of farming, in the homeland region of the Anatolian farmers' ancestors.","sites":"G\u00f6bekli Tepe, Karahan Tepe, Saybur\u00e7, Neval\u0131 \u00c7ori, Ta\u015f Tepeler project sites","genetics":"Pre-Pottery Neolithic Anatolian populations broadly ancestral to the later ANF lineage that founded European farming (direct aDNA from these specific sites is still limited)","certainty":"reconstructed"},{"era":"Anatolian Neolithic farmers / Fertile Crescent","start":-8500,"end":-6000,"title":"The Anatolian Wellspring of Farming","blurb":"The farming ancestry that ultimately reached Neolithic Britain arose in central and western Anatolia and the wider northern Fertile Crescent. At sites like \u00c7atalh\u00f6y\u00fck people lived in dense roof-entered houses, set bull skulls and reliefs into their walls, and buried their dead beneath the floors. They domesticated cereals, sheep, goats and cattle, then carried that package and their genes across the Aegean and Mediterranean into Europe. Ancient DNA shows their spread into the Balkans and beyond largely replaced the local forager gene pool, though hunter-gatherer ancestry persisted and later partly resurged on the continent. These Anatolian farmers are the genetic mother-population of Britain's first farmers.","sites":"\u00c7atalh\u00f6y\u00fck, A\u015f\u0131kl\u0131 H\u00f6y\u00fck, Boncuklu, Aegean coastal Neolithic sites","genetics":"Anatolian Neolithic Farmer (ANF), source of European EEF ancestry; their expansion largely replaced (but did not fully erase) Balkan forager ancestry en route to Europe","certainty":"documented"},{"era":"Neolithic Britain (Early Farmers)","start":-4000,"end":-2450,"title":"Stone Circles and the First Farmers","blurb":"The first farmers reached Britain around 4000 BCE, descended mainly from Aegean/Anatolian Neolithic stock who had taken the Mediterranean route across Europe, with a minor hunter-gatherer admixture picked up on the continent. They cleared forest for wheat, barley and cattle, raised long barrows and chambered tombs, and built great ceremonial landscapes \u2014 Skara Brae with its stone furniture, the Ness of Brodgar complex, and the first earthwork-and-bluestone phase of Stonehenge. Ancient DNA shows they largely replaced Britain's Mesolithic foragers, and that hunter-gatherer ancestry never strongly resurged here. They likely had darker skin and dark hair and eyes on average, though pigmentation varied.","sites":"Skara Brae, Ness of Brodgar, Stonehenge (earthwork phase), West Kennet Long Barrow, Avebury","genetics":"~75-80% Aegean/Anatolian Early-European-Farmer ancestry with ~20-25% Western Hunter-Gatherer admixture acquired on the continent; non-resurging WHG; generally darker pigmentation","certainty":"documented"},{"era":"Bell Beaker Britain (Early Bronze Age)","start":-2450,"end":-800,"title":"The Beaker Transformation and the Coming of Bronze","blurb":"From around 2450 BCE, Beaker-using migrants reached Britain from the Lower Rhine and adjacent continental Europe, bringing bell-shaped pots, copper and then bronze metallurgy, archery kit, and single burials under round barrows. Ancient DNA records one of Europe's most dramatic population turnovers: roughly 90% of Britain's gene pool was replaced over a few centuries, introducing steppe-derived ancestry and the Y-haplogroup R1b that dominates Britain today. The Neolithic farmers who had built Stonehenge faded from the gene pool even as the monument remained in use. This steppe-rich ancestry is the largest single component of modern British and Irish DNA.","sites":"Amesbury Archer, Stonehenge (sarsen phase), Boscombe Bowmen, round barrows on Salisbury Plain","genetics":"~90% gene-pool turnover (Olalde et al. 2018); large steppe (Yamnaya-derived) component; Y-DNA R1b-P312/L21 dominant; carried lighter-pigmentation alleles","certainty":"documented"},{"era":"Insular Celtic / Late Iron Age Britain","start":-800,"end":410,"title":"Hillforts, Druids and the Celtic Tongue","blurb":"Across Britain and Ireland, Iron Age communities spoke Brittonic and Goidelic Celtic languages, built hillforts like Maiden Castle, and produced the flowing La T\u00e8ne art seen on the Battersea Shield and Snettisham torcs. They worked iron, minted coins, and made votive offerings at watery places. Ancient DNA indicates this 'Celtic' identity spread mainly through culture and contact rather than mass migration: the population was largely descended from the Bronze Age Beaker-derived people, with a real but more modest gene flow from continental Europe into southern Britain in the Late Bronze and Iron Age. This is the Celtic stratum strongest today in Wales, Cornwall, Ireland and the Scottish Highlands.","sites":"Maiden Castle, Danebury, Snettisham hoard, Battersea Shield","genetics":"Predominantly Bronze Age Beaker-derived descent; substantial Late Bronze/Iron Age continental (EEF-rich) gene flow into southern Britain (Patterson et al. 2021) associated with rising lactase persistence","certainty":"well-supported"},{"era":"Anglo-Saxon & Romano-British England","start":410,"end":1066,"title":"The Mixing of Britons and Saxons","blurb":"After Roman administration ended around 410 CE, the lowland Britons absorbed migrants from across the North Sea \u2014 Angles, Saxons, Jutes and Frisians \u2014 with later Norse and Danish settlement in the Danelaw. They farmed, buried people with seaxes and shields, raised timber halls, and built the kingdoms of Wessex, Mercia and Northumbria. The 2022 Gretzinger study of early-medieval cemeteries found continental northern-European ancestry averaging roughly 75% in eastern England but varying widely by site and individual, mixing with the resident Romano-British population rather than replacing it. This fusion shaped English and the genetic baseline of lowland England.","sites":"Sutton Hoo, West Heslerton, Buckland (Dover), Mucking","genetics":"Continental North Sea / northern-European ancestry, on average ~75% in eastern England (Gretzinger et al. 2022), highly variable, layered over a Romano-British/Iron-Age base; Norse input in the Danelaw","certainty":"documented"}]},"germanic":{"label":"Germanic & Western Europe","dnaPct":21,"cards":[{"era":"Upper Palaeolithic Europeans (Aurignacian\u2013Gravettian\u2013Magdalenian)","start":-45000,"end":-15000,"title":"First modern humans of Ice Age Europe","blurb":"The earliest Homo sapiens to settle Ice Age Europe \u2014 Aurignacian toolmakers, Gravettian mammoth-hunters who built mammoth-bone dwellings and carved 'Venus' figurines, and Magdalenian artists who painted Lascaux and Altamira. Their ancestors had already interbred with Neanderthals, the source of the ~2% Neanderthal DNA every non-African person, Germanic descendants included, still carries. Through repeated glacial bottlenecks and re-expansions from southern refuges, their lineages fed into the Western Hunter-Gatherers who reoccupied the north \u2014 the deepest knowable ancestral layer of the Germanic gene pool, the foragers from whom everything else descends or into whom it later married.","sites":"Lascaux & Chauvet caves, Doln\u00ed V\u011bstonice (Gravettian), Sungir, Hohle Fels (Venus & flute), Goyet","genetics":"Founding European modern-human lineages; ~2% Neanderthal admixture (inherited from earlier interbreeding); ancestral via Magdalenian/post-glacial refugia to the WHG strand","certainty":"reconstructed"},{"era":"Western Hunter-Gatherers (WHG) \u2014 post-glacial Europe","start":-15000,"end":-7000,"title":"The foragers of the reforesting north","blurb":"As the ice sheets retreated, a broadly related population of hunter-gatherers spread across Europe, from Iberia to the Baltic, tracking red deer, aurochs and salmon through regrowing forest. Genomes from individuals like Cheddar Man and Loschbour carry dark-pigmentation gene variants together with alleles for pale eyes \u2014 the basis for the widely-shared 'dark-skinned, blue-eyed' image, though the actual skin tone is now treated cautiously: those predictions rest on incomplete data and the genetics of pigmentation are more complex than first reported. In the Lower Rhine and Low Countries these WHG foragers proved unusually resilient, surviving alongside and partly mixing with incoming farmers, which is why later Dutch and NW German Beaker people carry so much forager DNA. They are the indigenous European strand braided into every Germanic genome.","sites":"Loschbour (Luxembourg), Cheddar Gorge (UK), Hardinxveld-Giessendam & Rhine\u2013Meuse forager sites (NL)","genetics":"Western Hunter-Gatherer ancestry, Y-haplogroup I2, mtDNA U5; carries dark-skin and light-eye variants (exact pigmentation uncertain); the WHG strand surviving heavily in Lower Rhine Beaker populations","certainty":"well-supported"},{"era":"Ta\u015f Tepeler / Karahan Tepe \u2014 Pre-Pottery Neolithic of Upper Mesopotamia","start":-9500,"end":-8000,"title":"Stone-Hill sites at the threshold of farming","blurb":"On the \u015eanl\u0131urfa plain of SE Turkey, the Ta\u015f Tepeler ('Stone Hills') research program \u2014 a coordinated effort across roughly a dozen sites \u2014 has reshaped the picture of the early Neolithic. Karahan Tepe is no lone shrine but a substantial settlement of about 10 hectares (more if its pillar quarries are counted), with rock-cut chambers including one of phallic pillars watched over by a carved human head, and the so-called ribcage 'Corpse Statue.' Its sister G\u00f6bekli Tepe yields not only T-pillar enclosures but hearths, cisterns and signs of domestic life. The 2025 season added the first T-pillar bearing a carved human face, taken as evidence the pillars represent people. The decisive point holds: hunter-gatherers raised monumental T-shaped pillars and built dense communities before full farming, complicating the old 'farming first, monuments later' order. These are cultural forerunners of the Anatolian farmers whose ancestry later reaches NW Europe, faintly but really.","sites":"G\u00f6bekli Tepe, Karahan Tepe, Saybur\u00e7, Sefer Tepe, \u00c7akmaktepe, G\u00fcrc\u00fctepe (Ta\u015f Tepeler project)","genetics":"Pre-Pottery Neolithic Upper Mesopotamian / Anatolian foragers-turning-farmers; ancestral to the broader Anatolian Neolithic lineage that helps seed Europe's EEF (direct aDNA from these specific sites remains limited)","certainty":"well-supported"},{"era":"Aegean & western Anatolian Neolithic \u2014 \u00c7atalh\u00f6y\u00fck and the springboard west","start":-7100,"end":-6000,"title":"The farmers who would sail west","blurb":"As farming matured in central and western Anatolia, \u00c7atalh\u00f6y\u00fck rose on the Konya plain (c. 7100\u20136000 BC) \u2014 a town of mudbrick houses packed wall-to-wall and entered through the roof, where people set bulls' horns into installations and buried their dead beneath the floors. From these western Anatolian and Aegean farming communities came the population that, generations on, carried wheat, sheep and the farming way of life across the Aegean into Greece and up the Danube. Ancient DNA ties Europe's first farmers directly back to this Anatolian source: it is the genetic and cultural launch-pad for the LBK, and thus for the farmer share of Germanic ancestry. (Earlier central-Anatolian villages such as A\u015f\u0131kl\u0131 H\u00f6y\u00fck, c. 8400 BC, set the stage before \u00c7atalh\u00f6y\u00fck.)","sites":"\u00c7atalh\u00f6y\u00fck, A\u015f\u0131kl\u0131 H\u00f6y\u00fck, Boncuklu, Barc\u0131n H\u00f6y\u00fck, Aegean Neolithic (Greece)","genetics":"Anatolian Neolithic Farmer ancestry \u2014 the direct genetic source of Europe's Early European Farmers (Mathieson 2015; Hofmanov\u00e1 2016)","certainty":"documented"},{"era":"Linearbandkeramik (LBK) \u2014 first farmers of central Europe","start":-5500,"end":-4900,"title":"The first farmers reach the loess","blurb":"The first farming villages of central Europe spread quickly along fertile loess soils from Hungary to the Rhine and Paris Basin, sharing one signature: massive timber longhouses, linear-banded pottery, polished stone adzes, and emmer wheat, barley, cattle, pigs and sheep with Near Eastern roots. They cleared forest, lived in tight hamlets, and occasionally erupted in lethal violence (the Talheim and Sch\u00f6neck-Kilianst\u00e4dten massacre pits). Ancient DNA shows the LBK were not converted locals but incoming farmers descended from Anatolian/Aegean populations \u2014 the 'Early European Farmer' (EEF) layer that, mixed with native foragers, forms one of the three great strands of all later European ancestry, including the Germanic.","sites":"Talheim & Sch\u00f6neck-Kilianst\u00e4dten death pits, Vaihingen, Asparn-Schletz, Elsloo (NL) longhouse villages","genetics":"Early European Farmer (Anatolian-derived) ancestry, Y-haplogroup G2a, frequent mtDNA N1a; the EEF base under all later NW-European genomes (Haak 2015; Mathieson 2018)","certainty":"documented"},{"era":"Corded Ware complex (steppe arrival in central Europe)","start":-2900,"end":-2350,"title":"The steppe pours into the north","blurb":"Across a vast band from the Rhine to the Volga, mobile pastoralists appeared within a few centuries, burying their dead crouched under barrows with corded-decorated pots and stone battle-axes. They brought a large new ancestry derived from the Yamnaya herders of the Pontic-Caspian steppe \u2014 which replaced much of the male gene pool in parts of central and northern Europe \u2014 along with Indo-European language ancestral to Germanic. The northern variants (Single Grave in Germany/Denmark, Battle Axe in Scandinavia) are the bedrock beneath every later Germanic population. Recent work nuances the old 'pure Yamnaya invasion' story: Corded Ware men carry mostly R1a (R1a-M417) rather than Yamnaya's R1b, so they descend from a parallel steppe-derived population, not directly from Yamnaya. The lactase-persistence allele was present but still rare here, only rising to high frequency much later.","sites":"Corded Ware/Single Grave barrows across N Germany & Denmark, Battle Axe graves (Scandinavia), Eulau mass grave","genetics":"Large Western Steppe Herder (Yamnaya-related) ancestry over an EEF substrate; predominantly R1a-M417 Y-lineages; vector of Indo-European speech; lactase-persistence allele present but low-frequency (Haak 2015; later revisions)","certainty":"documented"},{"era":"Single Grave / Bell Beaker culture (NW Europe)","start":-2800,"end":-1800,"title":"The Beaker remix of the Rhine and the Low Countries","blurb":"In the wetlands and sand-ridges of the Netherlands, Belgium and western Germany, steppe-descended Corded Ware herders met an unusually persistent resident population of hunter-fishers. The 2026 Olalde study (112 genomes from the Rhine\u2013Meuse delta, Nature) showed that this lowland forager population kept roughly 50% hunter-gatherer ancestry \u2014 far more than elsewhere in Europe \u2014 right up to about 2500 BC. When steppe-derived newcomers arrived, the two mixed: the resulting Bell Beaker groups carried R1b male lineages and the bell-cup, single-grave, copper-dagger and wrist-guard package, with the local foragers contributing roughly 13\u201318% of their ancestry. These Single Grave and Beaker folk are direct ancestral stock of the Nordic Bronze Age and the later Germanic world.","sites":"Rhine\u2013Meuse delta cemeteries (NL/Belgium/W Germany), Veluwe Beaker barrows, Oostwoud","genetics":"Steppe (Yamnaya-related) ancestry blended with exceptionally persistent Lower Rhine forager ancestry (~50% locally until c. 2500 BC; ~13\u201318% in the resulting Beaker groups) plus EEF; R1b-dominated Y-lineages (Olalde 2018; Olalde et al., Nature 2026)","certainty":"documented"},{"era":"Nordic Bronze Age","start":-1700,"end":-500,"title":"Sun-disc and bronze on the northern plain","blurb":"A brilliant Bronze Age culture flowered across Denmark, southern Scandinavia and the adjacent North German coast \u2014 people with no local copper or tin who imported it in quantity, casting lurs (curved horns), swords and the gilded Trundholm sun-chariot. They raised thousands of turf burial mounds over oak-coffin chiefs (the Egtved Girl, c. 1370 BC, among them), carved ships and sun-symbols into rock, and ran amber-for-metal trade along routes that reached the Mediterranean world. Most scholars treat this as the cultural reservoir ancestral to the later Germanic peoples. DNA shows a population formed from Corded Ware / Single Grave and Bell Beaker roots, carrying heavy steppe ancestry \u2014 the same blend the later Jastorf and Saxon-Frisian world inherited.","sites":"Trundholm sun-chariot, Egtved Girl & Borum Esh\u00f8j oak-coffin graves, Tanum rock-art, Hjortspring","genetics":"Single Grave / Battle Axe + Bell Beaker derived; high steppe ancestry on an EEF+WHG base; broadly continuous into Iron Age Germanic populations","certainty":"well-supported"},{"era":"Roman Iron Age & Jastorf culture","start":-600,"end":400,"title":"Where the Germanic peoples crystallize","blurb":"On the North German Plain between Elbe and Oder, the Jastorf culture marks the point where the Germanic-speaking world becomes archaeologically visible \u2014 iron-using villagers who cremated their dead in urnfields, farmed emmer and barley, and herded cattle on sandy heath. As Rome reached the Rhine and Danube, these peoples enter the record as Cherusci, Chauci and early Saxons and Frisians, described in Tacitus's Germania. They traded amber and hides for Roman bronze and wine, fought Rome at the Teutoburg Forest, and slowly coalesced into the Migration-Period confederations. Genetically they sit on a continuum with earlier Nordic Bronze Age and Corded Ware populations, with no sharp break \u2014 'Germanic' is a cultural-linguistic label on a long-resident northern gene pool.","sites":"Jastorf type-site (Lower Saxony), Kalkriese (Teutoburg battlefield), Hjortspring & Illerup weapon-deposits","genetics":"Continuity from Nordic Bronze Age stock; standard NW-European steppe/EEF/WHG mix; no major population replacement \u2014 an in-situ cultural emergence","certainty":"well-supported"},{"era":"Continental Germanic peoples (Franks, Saxons, Frisians)","start":400,"end":800,"title":"The North Sea coast people","blurb":"Iron-working farmers and seafarers of the great river deltas and heath \u2014 Franks along the Rhine, Saxons between Weser and Elbe, Frisians on the terpen mounds they raised above the salt marshes. They lived in long timber halls shared with their cattle and spoke the early West Germanic dialects that became Dutch, Low German and English. Ancient DNA from the 2022 Gretzinger study (460 medieval NW Europeans) shows a coherent 'continental North Sea' gene pool stretching from the northern Netherlands through northern Germany to Denmark; early medieval eastern English individuals drew up to ~76% of their ancestry from it, the genetic signature of large-scale family migration across the sea. They carried the language, the longhouse, and a coastal cattle-and-trade way of life.","sites":"Frisian terpen (Ezinge, Feddersen Wierde), Saxon Wijster, Anglo-Saxon cemeteries of eastern England","genetics":"'Continental North Sea' / CNE cluster (Gretzinger et al., Nature 2022); typical NW-European steppe+EEF+WHG blend; source of up to ~76% of early-medieval eastern English ancestry","certainty":"documented"}]},"nordic":{"label":"The Norse North","dnaPct":18,"cards":[{"era":"Western Hunter-Gatherers & the post-glacial recolonization","start":-15000,"end":-9500,"title":"Following the Ice North","blurb":"As the great ice sheets retreated, dark-skinned, blue-eyed Western Hunter-Gatherers (WHG) spread up through a re-greening Europe while a related eastern line tracked the forest-steppe. These were the populations that later fed into the SHG. They lived in small mobile bands hunting reindeer, aurochs and red deer, and sit on the western side of the 'great divide' \u2014 a sharp Black-Sea-to-Baltic genetic boundary mapped in the 2024 post-glacial Eurasia study. They are the oldest layer whose direct genetic signal still survives in modern Scandinavians.","sites":"Bromme & late-glacial Ahrensburgian sites (DK), Hensbacka coastal sites (SE), broader Ahrensburgian/Maglemosian horizon","genetics":"WHG cluster west of the 'great divide'; a founding contributor to SHG ancestry (Allentoft et al. 2024, post-glacial western Eurasia, Nature).","certainty":"well-supported"},{"era":"Pre-Pottery Neolithic Anatolia / Upper Mesopotamia \u2014 Tas Tepeler","start":-9600,"end":-8000,"title":"Monuments Before the Harvest \u2014 Gobekli & Karahan Tepe","blurb":"The far-upstream farmer source. At Gobekli Tepe and the broadly contemporaneous Karahan Tepe \u2014 part of the ~12-site Tas Tepeler program in southeast Turkey \u2014 people raised T-shaped megalithic pillars carved with animals while still living largely on wild foods. Recent digs reinforced 'settlement, not only sanctuary': domestic structures and grinding stones sit alongside the monuments, and in 2025 the first T-shaped pillar bearing a carved human face was found at Karahan Tepe. The monuments came before full farming, not after. Their descendants' Anatolian-farmer ancestry was carried, leg by leg, toward the dolmen-builders of the Nordic shore.","sites":"Gobekli Tepe, Karahan Tepe, Sayburc, wider Tas Tepeler sites (Sanliurfa, Turkey)","genetics":"Pre-Pottery Neolithic Anatolians = a blend of local Epipalaeolithic Anatolian and Levantine/Mesopotamian-related sources; ancestral to the Anatolian-farmer component later seen in TRB Scandinavia (Lazaridis et al. 2022; Altinisik et al. 2022).","certainty":"well-supported"},{"era":"Scandinavian Mesolithic & Pitted Ware hunter-gatherers","start":-9500,"end":-4000,"title":"The SHG \u2014 Foragers of the Drowned Coast","blurb":"The deep native layer: post-glacial hunter-fisher-gatherers who recolonized Scandinavia from two directions, blending Western Hunter-Gatherers from the south with an Eastern/Ancient-North-Eurasian line from the northeast into the distinctive 'Scandinavian Hunter-Gatherer' (SHG) genome. Unlike the dark-skinned WHG to their south, the SHG carried a mosaic of eye colours and lighter skin. They speared seals, fished, and left tools and birch-bark mastic chewed with their own DNA still in it. The later Pitted Ware people of Sweden were a striking case of farmers' descendants reverting to a sea-mammal-hunting forager life.","sites":"Motala (SE), Stora Forvar & Ajvide (Pitted Ware, Gotland), Hensbacka/Sandarna, Tybrind Vig (DK)","genetics":"SHG = WHG (south) + Eastern/ANE (northeast) admixture; mixed eye colour and lighter skin than WHG; chewed-mastic DNA links culture to genetics (Gunther et al. 2018; Kashuba et al. 2019).","certainty":"documented"},{"era":"Funnelbeaker culture (TRB)","start":-4000,"end":-2800,"title":"First Farmers, Megaliths, and the Plough","blurb":"Scandinavia's first farmers: they cleared forest for emmer wheat and barley, herded cattle, made funnel-necked pottery, and raised the great dolmens and passage graves still standing across Denmark and southern Sweden. DNA shows a strong infusion of Neolithic Anatolian-farmer ancestry arriving in the North around 5,900 years ago \u2014 an abrupt turnover with limited input from local hunter-gatherers, the first of the two replacements documented in Denmark. They passed down domesticated crops and livestock, monumental collective tombs, and the settled village.","sites":"Sarup causewayed enclosures, Poskaer Stenhus & countless dolmens (DK), passage graves of Falbygden (SE)","genetics":"Major Neolithic Anatolian-farmer ancestry largely replacing the local hunter-gatherer profile; first turnover in the 100-genome Neolithic Denmark study (Allentoft et al. 2024).","certainty":"documented"},{"era":"Pontic-Caspian Steppe pastoralists (Yamnaya source)","start":-3300,"end":-2600,"title":"Horse, Wheel, and the Steppe Inheritance","blurb":"The eastern parent of the Battle-Axe layer. On the grasslands north of the Black and Caspian Seas, Yamnaya herders \u2014 themselves a fusion of Eastern Hunter-Gatherers with Caucasus and Lower-Volga ancestry \u2014 drove cattle, used wagons, and buried their dead under kurgan mounds. Within centuries their genes, and very likely an early Indo-European tongue, surged across Europe into Scandinavia via Corded Ware. They are the ultimate source of roughly a third of modern Nordic ancestry and a major root of the Germanic language family.","sites":"Yamnaya kurgans (Pontic-Caspian steppe), Khvalynsk & Lower Volga precursor cemeteries","genetics":"EHG + Caucasus-hunter-gatherer / Lower-Volga ancestry; roughly a third of modern North-European autosomal ancestry derives from this steppe source (Allentoft et al. 2024; recent steppe-origins work).","certainty":"well-supported"},{"era":"Battle-Axe / Corded Ware culture","start":-2800,"end":-2300,"title":"The Steppe Arrives \u2014 Cord, Axe, and a New Tongue","blurb":"The Scandinavian branch (Single Grave / Battle-Axe culture) of the pan-European Corded Ware horizon. Mobile cattle-herders buried under single barrows with cord-decorated beakers and polished stone battle-axes. They carried a large new genetic component \u2014 on the order of three-quarters of their ancestry traceable to Pontic-Caspian steppe (Yamnaya-related) pastoralists \u2014 that swept into Denmark within a few generations, the sharper of the two turnovers in the 100-genome Neolithic Denmark study. They very likely brought early Indo-European speech, ancestral to the later Germanic languages.","sites":"Single Grave barrows (Jutland), Swedish Battle-Axe burials, Linkoping-area graves","genetics":"Large Pontic-Caspian Steppe (Yamnaya-related) ancestry pulse (~3/4) replacing much of the prior farmer gene-pool; the second turnover in Allentoft et al. 2024 (Neolithic Denmark).","certainty":"documented"},{"era":"Nordic Bronze Age","start":-1700,"end":-500,"title":"Sun-Disc, Bronze, and the Painted Ships","blurb":"A wealthy, sea-facing chiefdom culture that imported tin and copper from the Alps and Britain and exported Baltic amber. They cast the Trundholm sun chariot, hammered the Tanum rock-art panels full of ships and sun-symbols, and buried elites under turf mounds in oak-log coffins (Egtved Girl, Borum Eshoj). Genetically they already carried the classic Nordic three-way blend, stabilized after the steppe arrival. They passed down a solar cosmology, monumental burial, and a maritime economy that the later Norse inherited.","sites":"Trundholm sun chariot (DK), Tanum & Kivik rock art (SE), Egtved Girl & Borum Eshoj oak-coffin burials (DK)","genetics":"Stabilized WHG-farmer-Steppe mix; strong continuity into the Iron Age and modern Scandinavians (Allentoft et al. 2024; Nordic Bronze Age genomic studies).","certainty":"well-supported"},{"era":"Viking Age & Germanic Iron Age Norse","start":-100,"end":1050,"title":"Sail, Saga, and the Northern Reach","blurb":"Iron-Age Norse farmers, traders and raiders who turned the North and Baltic Seas into highways. They built clinker-hulled longships, raised runestones, smithed pattern-welded swords, and farmed barley and cattle around longhouses. The 442-genome Viking-world study showed Scandinavia was no closed gene-pool: it took in ancestry from southern and eastern Europe during this period and projected Danish ancestry into England, Swedish into the Baltic, and Norwegian into Ireland, Iceland and Greenland. It also showed 'Viking' was a way of life, not a single bloodline.","sites":"Jelling royal mounds & runestones (DK), Birka (SE), Uppakra, Lindholm Hoje, Oseberg & Gokstad ships, Hedeby/Haithabu","genetics":"Modern North-Germanic baseline: roughly a 3-way mix of WHG-rich forager, Neolithic Anatolian-farmer and Steppe ancestry, with Viking-Age inflow from southern/eastern Europe (Margaryan et al. 2020, Nature).","certainty":"documented"}]},"centraleast":{"label":"Central & Eastern Europe","dnaPct":10,"cards":[{"era":"Pre-Pottery Neolithic \u2014 Ta\u015f Tepeler (G\u00f6bekli Tepe & Karahan Tepe)","start":-9500,"end":-8000,"title":"Monuments Before the Harvest","blurb":"In the limestone hills of Upper Mesopotamia, early Neolithic communities raised some of the oldest monumental architecture on Earth \u2014 rings and rooms of carved T-shaped pillars bearing foxes, vultures, snakes and bulls. The Ta\u015f Tepeler ('Stone Hills') program, a cluster of related sites, has reshaped the old 'temple in an empty landscape' picture: Karahan Tepe shows real settlement alongside its ritual buildings, including the striking Structure AB with eleven bedrock-carved pillars and a human head emerging from the rock. Communal gathering and feasting, as much as farming, may have pulled people together \u2014 with agriculture following. Only a small fraction of Karahan Tepe has been excavated.","sites":"G\u00f6bekli Tepe; Karahan Tepe (Structure AB pillar room; carved heads; a newly identified communal building, 2024); Saybur\u00e7, Sefer Tepe and the wider Ta\u015f Tepeler cluster (SE T\u00fcrkiye)","genetics":"No published genomes from these specific PPN sites yet, but they sit within the Upper-Mesopotamian/Anatolian Neolithic world whose ancestry \u2014 blended with central-Anatolian foragers \u2014 fed the farmer signal carried into Europe. A shared deep human horizon.","certainty":"reconstructed"},{"era":"Aceramic Neolithic Anatolia (Boncuklu & \u00c7atalh\u00f6y\u00fck precursors)","start":-8500,"end":-7000,"title":"The Source of the Farmer Ancestry","blurb":"At small mound-villages like Boncuklu H\u00f6y\u00fck on the Konya plain, indigenous central-Anatolian foragers began cultivating cereals and tending early herds while still hunting and gathering \u2014 close to the ancestral communities whose descendants carried farming, and their genes, into Europe. Their tightly packed mud-brick houses, entered through the roof and replastered for generations, prefigure the famous town of \u00c7atalh\u00f6y\u00fck nearby. Almost every Central European carries a thread reaching back to people like these.","sites":"Boncuklu H\u00f6y\u00fck (Konya plain); \u00c7atalh\u00f6y\u00fck; A\u015f\u0131kl\u0131 H\u00f6y\u00fck; central Anatolian aceramic villages","genetics":"Represents 'Anatolian Neolithic Farmer' ancestry \u2014 the population genetically ancestral to LBK and Europe's early farmers; largely continuous with local central-Anatolian forager ancestry and distinct from the Levant.","certainty":"documented"},{"era":"Linearbandkeramik (LBK) \u2014 first Central European farmers","start":-5500,"end":-4900,"title":"The Longhouse Pioneers","blurb":"The first farmers of the loess plains, the Linear Pottery people, spread quickly from the middle Danube to the Rhine and Vistula, raising great timber longhouses, planting emmer and einkorn, herding cattle, and decorating pots with incised linear bands. Ancient DNA shows they were not local foragers who 'learned' farming but a migrating population carrying Anatolian-derived ancestry, who lived alongside \u2014 and only slowly mixed with \u2014 the indigenous hunter-gatherers they met.","sites":"Longhouse villages across Bohemia, Moravia and Poland; Talheim death-pit (early Neolithic violence); large LBK genome syntheses","genetics":"Strong Anatolian Neolithic ancestry, ultimately derived from central-Anatolian farming communities; modest Western Hunter-Gatherer admixture rising over time; mtDNA N1a a recurring marker \u2014 the farmer layer beneath the later steppe overlay.","certainty":"documented"},{"era":"Cucuteni\u2013Trypillia culture","start":-4800,"end":-3000,"title":"The Mega-Sites That Burned Their Houses","blurb":"On the forest-steppe of present Ukraine, Moldova and Romania, Trypillian farmers built some of the largest settlements of their day \u2014 planned, ring-shaped 'mega-sites' like Talianki and Maidanetske with hundreds to thousands of houses (population estimates are debated, from a few thousand up to higher figures). They painted spiral pottery in red, white and black, modelled clay figurines, and repeatedly burned houses \u2014 possibly in deliberate cycles of renewal, though the practice is still debated. Their forest-steppe world bordered and interacted with the populations that fed into the steppe sphere.","sites":"Talianki, Maidanetske, Nebelivka mega-sites (Ukraine); painted-ware and burnt-house horizons; figurine assemblages","genetics":"Mostly Anatolian-farmer ancestry with a notable Eastern/steppe hunter-gatherer component along the forest-steppe frontier \u2014 a genetic bridge between Old-European farmers and the emerging steppe world.","certainty":"well-supported"},{"era":"Yamnaya / Western Steppe Herders","start":-3300,"end":-2600,"title":"Wagons, Herds and the Indo-European Spark","blurb":"On the Pontic-Caspian grasslands north of the Black Sea, the Yamnaya ('pit-grave') herders lived from ox-drawn wagon and a mobile cattle-and-sheep economy, raising kurgans over ochre-stained dead and ranging across vast territories. Their rapid expansion \u2014 one of the largest demographic events in Eurasian prehistory \u2014 carried steppe ancestry and an early Indo-European language deep into Europe, supplying the engine behind Corded Ware. This is the steppe thread that still runs strong through Central & Eastern European DNA.","sites":"Kurgan fields of the Ukrainian/Russian steppe; Pontic-Caspian pit-graves; ochre burials with wagon parts","genetics":"Defines 'Western Steppe Herder' ancestry \u2014 roughly a mix of Eastern Hunter-Gatherer and Caucasus Hunter-Gatherer ancestry; Y-haplogroup R1b-Z2103; about half the autosomal makeup of later Central/East European populations traces here.","certainty":"documented"},{"era":"Globular Amphora culture","start":-3100,"end":-2700,"title":"The Last Farmers Before the Steppe","blurb":"On the eve of the steppe expansion, the Globular Amphora people farmed the Polish and Bohemian lowlands, known for round-bellied amphorae, cattle herding, and elaborate ritual deposits \u2014 including cattle buried with care and the harrowing mass grave at Koszyce, where an extended family was killed and then laid out side by side, kin next to kin, by survivors. Genetically they were still overwhelmingly old-European farmers, with very little steppe ancestry \u2014 a population on the verge of being transformed.","sites":"Koszyce mass grave (S Poland); cattle-burial sites across Poland/Bohemia; cist and megalithic graves","genetics":"Predominantly Anatolian-Neolithic-farmer ancestry with significant Western Hunter-Gatherer admixture; steppe ancestry minimal \u2014 a clean 'before' snapshot against which the Corded Ware shift is measured.","certainty":"documented"},{"era":"Corded Ware culture (Single Grave)","start":-2900,"end":-2300,"title":"Battle-Axes and the Steppe Inheritance","blurb":"Corded Ware spread rapidly across the North-European plain: single crouched burials under low mounds, men with stone battle-axes, women with cord-impressed beakers, a mobile herding life of cattle and sheep with wagons. Ancient DNA revealed a genuine, large infusion of new ancestry \u2014 incomers carrying a high fraction of Yamnaya-related steppe ancestry, overwhelmingly Y-haplogroup R1a-M417, the deep paternal root of most modern Polish and Czech men. With them came new burial ideology and almost certainly early Indo-European speech.","sites":"Single-grave barrows across Poland & Czechia; SE Poland CWC cemeteries (isotope + genome studies); Battle-Axe sites of the N European plain","genetics":"High Western Steppe Herder (Yamnaya-related) autosomal ancestry (commonly ~50\u201375% in early CWC individuals); Y-DNA dominated by R1a-M417 (NOT Yamnaya's R1b) \u2014 direct ancestor signal for NE Poland/E Czechia; maternal lines partly retain older Neolithic-farmer mtDNA.","certainty":"documented"},{"era":"\u00dan\u011btice / Trzciniec Bronze Age","start":-2200,"end":-1500,"title":"Bronze, Hoards and the First Princes","blurb":"With the steppe-derived population now settled, the Early Bronze Age \u00dan\u011btice culture cast central Europe's first true tin-bronze \u2014 daggers, halberds, ingot-torcs \u2014 and buried elites under barrows with fine metalwork, the milieu that produced the Nebra Sky Disc just west of here. In NE Poland the related Trzciniec tradition carried the same world of barrows and bronze along the forest edge. Long-distance tin and amber networks knit Bohemia, Saxony and Poland into one prestige economy.","sites":"\u00dan\u011btice barrow and hoard sites (Bohemia, Silesia, Saxony); Nebra Sky Disc context (Saxony-Anhalt); Trzciniec barrows (E/NE Poland)","genetics":"Direct continuation of Corded Ware / Bell Beaker populations \u2014 roughly half Western Steppe Herder ancestry blended with Neolithic-farmer and hunter-gatherer ancestry; the stabilized base on which all later layers sit.","certainty":"well-supported"},{"era":"Iron Age Central Europe (Przeworsk culture)","start":-300,"end":400,"title":"Smiths, Warriors and the Roman Frontier","blurb":"Across southern and central Poland the Przeworsk culture smelted bog-iron into swords and tools on a remarkable scale, cremated their dead with deliberately bent and 'killed' weapons, and traded amber down the rivers to the Roman frontier for wine, glass and silver coin. Long linked loosely to the Vandals and other East-Germanic groups, Przeworsk people form part of the deep substrate that later Slavic incomers settled into and absorbed.","sites":"Iron-smelting fields of the \u015awi\u0119tokrzyskie (Holy Cross) Mountains; weapon-burial cemeteries across S Poland; Roman amber-road finds","genetics":"Northern-central-European Iron Age profile: substantial Bronze Age steppe plus earlier-farmer and hunter-gatherer ancestry; Iron Age Germanic-associated and later Slavic-associated groups overlap heavily on shared Baltic/Central-European Bronze Age ancestry, so they are hard to separate by PCA alone.","certainty":"well-supported"},{"era":"Early Medieval Slavs (Prague-Korchak horizon; Great Moravia)","start":500,"end":1000,"title":"The Wave That Remade a Continent","blurb":"From wetland villages of square sunken-floor huts and plain hand-built Prague-type pots, Slavic-speaking communities spread across what is now Poland, Czechia and the Elbe-Saale lands, farming millet and rye. By the 9th century they had coalesced into Great Moravia, with hillforts, churches and the Cyrillo-Methodian (Glagolitic) script. The 2025 HistoGenes ancient-DNA study (over 550 ancient genomes, Nature) showed this was no mere fashion in pottery: a real demographic movement out of Eastern Europe replaced more than 80% of the local gene pool in eastern Germany, Poland and Croatia, with whole families migrating (no sex bias).","sites":"Prague-Korchak settlements; Mikul\u010dice and Pohansko (Great Moravia, CZ); HistoGenes 2025 cemeteries across PL/CZ/DE/HR","genetics":"Arrival of an Eastern-European 'Slavic' cline (homeland between the Dniester and Don); >80% local replacement in E Germany/Poland/Croatia; Y-haplogroup R1a (M458/Z280 branches) becomes dominant \u2014 the modern E-Czech/NE-Polish signature.","certainty":"documented"}]},"balkan":{"label":"The Balkans & SE Europe","dnaPct":3,"cards":[{"era":"Ta\u015f Tepeler & the Pre-Pottery Neolithic of SE Anatolia","start":-9500,"end":-8000,"title":"Karahan Tepe & G\u00f6bekli Tepe \u2014 Monuments at the Dawn of Farming","blurb":"The deepest reachable root. Across the 'Stone Hills' (Ta\u015f Tepeler) of \u015eanl\u0131urfa, a network of sites \u2014 G\u00f6bekli Tepe, Karahan Tepe, Saybur\u00e7 and more \u2014 raised T-shaped megalithic pillars carved with foxes, vultures, snakes and human faces around the time farming was first taking hold, not long after. The old picture of these as empty temples visited only by nomads has been revised: excavators now read them as occupied settlements with domestic refuse, tools and rock-cut cisterns. Recent digs at Karahan Tepe have exposed rooms of carved phallic stelae and a striking life-size 'Ribbed Man' statue (c. 9400 BCE), among the oldest realistic human sculptures known. Here communal monument-building and shared belief ran alongside the earliest cultivation \u2014 the cultural seedbed of the people whose descendants became Europe's first farmers and, far downstream, this Balkan thread.","sites":"G\u00f6bekli Tepe; Karahan Tepe; Saybur\u00e7; the wider Ta\u015f Tepeler program (\u015eanl\u0131urfa, T\u00fcrkiye)","genetics":"Pre-Pottery Neolithic SE Anatolians broadly ancestral to the central-Anatolian farmer source; aDNA from these specific PPN sites is still sparse, so the link is inferred from cultural continuity and downstream genomes rather than direct sequencing.","certainty":"reconstructed"},{"era":"Anatolian source population \u2014 central-plateau Neolithic","start":-8300,"end":-6500,"title":"The Homeland: Boncuklu, \u00c7atalh\u00f6y\u00fck, A\u015f\u0131kl\u0131","blurb":"Before they were Europeans they were Anatolians. On the central plateau, villages like A\u015f\u0131kl\u0131 H\u00f6y\u00fck, Boncuklu and the famous honeycomb town of \u00c7atalh\u00f6y\u00fck grew dense and permanent \u2014 mudbrick houses entered through the roof, the dead buried beneath the floors, bulls and ancestral imagery on the walls. Ancient DNA shows these first farmers were overwhelmingly descendants of the local hunter-gatherers (~80-90% continuity) who took up farming in place, later seasoned by small inflows of Iranian/Caucasus- and Levant-related ancestry. A branch of these people became the Balkan founders.","sites":"\u00c7atalh\u00f6y\u00fck; A\u015f\u0131kl\u0131 H\u00f6y\u00fck; Boncuklu H\u00f6y\u00fck (c. 8300-7800 BCE); P\u0131narba\u015f\u0131 (Late-Pleistocene HG genome)","genetics":"Anatolian-Neolithic Farmer (ANF) source; ~80-90% continuity from local Anatolian hunter-gatherers; minor early Iranian/Caucasus + later Levantine admixture.","certainty":"documented"},{"era":"First Farmers of Europe \u2014 Star\u010devo, K\u00f6r\u00f6s, Karanovo","start":-6200,"end":-5400,"title":"The Farmers Who Made Europe","blurb":"This is the founding migration. Around 6200 BCE, people carrying Anatolian-Neolithic ancestry crossed into the Balkans with the full Neolithic package \u2014 wheat, barley, sheep, goats, cattle, pottery \u2014 and planted the first farming villages on European soil: Star\u010devo, K\u00f6r\u00f6s, Karanovo. They built tells (mounds raised by generations of mudbrick houses), and from here farming radiated up the Danube and across the Mediterranean to fill the continent. Their arrival replaced almost all of the local Balkan hunter-gatherer gene pool. Every European with farmer ancestry, including this Balkan/Albanian thread, traces back through them.","sites":"Star\u010devo (Serbia); Karanovo tell (Bulgaria); K\u00f6r\u00f6s sites (Hungary); early tells of Thessaly","genetics":"~Pure Anatolian-Neolithic Farmer ancestry (the 'EEF' source in all later Europeans); near-total (~98%) replacement of the local Balkan hunter-gatherer gene pool, with minimal initial HG admixture.","certainty":"documented"},{"era":"Balkan Copper Age \u2014 Vin\u010da & the gold of Varna","start":-5400,"end":-3500,"title":"Europe's First Goldsmiths and Town-Builders","blurb":"The farming population matured into one of prehistory's most dazzling cultures. The Vin\u010da built large planned settlements with substantial timber-and-daub houses and left tens of thousands of figurines plus the enigmatic scratched 'Vin\u010da signs' \u2014 an early symbol system often cited among the world's first proto-writing experiments (though not accepted as true writing). Around 4600-4200 BCE, graves at the Varna necropolis on the Black Sea held the oldest worked gold known on Earth \u2014 evidence of a ranked, wealthy society millennia before kings. Genetically these are still overwhelmingly Anatolian-farmer people, now with a little more local hunter-gatherer ancestry blended back in.","sites":"Vin\u010da-Belo Brdo; Plo\u010dnik (early copper metallurgy); Varna necropolis (~4600-4200 BCE); Karanovo VI / Gumelni\u021ba tells","genetics":"Predominantly Anatolian-Neolithic ancestry with rising Western-Hunter-Gatherer admixture through the Neolithic; pre-steppe.","certainty":"well-supported"},{"era":"Balkan Bronze Age \u2014 the steppe arrives","start":-2500,"end":-1200,"title":"Horsemen's Blood Joins the Farmers","blurb":"Around 2500 BCE the Balkan gene pool was rewritten a second time. People descended from the Yamnaya \u2014 herders off the Pontic-Caspian steppe, leading candidates for carrying early Indo-European speech \u2014 pushed in and mixed with the long-settled farming population. After this date western-Balkan men overwhelmingly carry the steppe Y-lineage R1b-Z2103. This farmer-plus-steppe fusion is the genetic foundation everything later sits on: the bedrock under the Illyrians, under Rome's Balkan subjects, and under modern Albania.","sites":"Cetina culture (Dalmatia); Vu\u010dedol-to-Bronze-Age transition; Reich Lab 'Southern Arc' sampling","genetics":"Major steppe (Yamnaya-related) admixture event from ~2500 BCE; arrival and rise of R1b-Z2103; a Yamnaya-like source fits better than Corded Ware or Bell Beaker.","certainty":"well-supported"},{"era":"Iron Age Balkans \u2014 Illyrians & Thracians","start":-1200,"end":-200,"title":"The Named Peoples: Illyrians and Thracians","blurb":"Now written history can name them. Greek and Roman writers describe Illyrian tribes across the western Balkans and Thracians to the east \u2014 hillfort-dwellers, metalworkers, raiders and traders sitting at the seam between the Mediterranean and the Eurasian interior. Ancient DNA places these populations between Early-Neolithic farmers and steppe pastoralists, confirming the Balkans as a long-standing bridge between East and West. Modern Albanians show strong paternal continuity with these Bronze-to-Iron-Age western-Balkan populations \u2014 the Illyrians of the textbooks.","sites":"Glasinac-Mati culture hillforts; Trebenishte; Thracian tumuli of Bulgaria; aDNA from Bronze/Iron Age Bulgaria & the western Balkans","genetics":"Intermediate Neolithic-farmer + steppe profile; western-Balkan males commonly carry Yamnaya-derived R1b-Z2103; aDNA supports paternal continuity into modern Albanians.","certainty":"well-supported"},{"era":"Roman Imperial Balkans \u2014 Illyricum & Thracia","start":-150,"end":500,"title":"Rome's Soldier-Province, Barely Roman by Blood","blurb":"For centuries the western Balkans were Roman Illyricum and Thracia \u2014 a hard frontier that bred legionaries and even emperors (Diocletian was born in Dalmatia; Constantine the Great at Naissus/Ni\u0161). Yet the DNA delivers a surprise: despite thorough political and cultural Romanization, almost no Italic ancestry arrived. The bigger genetic event of the imperial age was an influx of people carrying Anatolian-related ancestry, absorbed into local society. The Illyrian- and Thracian-descended population persisted under the Latin veneer, speaking ancestral tongues thought to feed into proto-Albanian.","sites":"Olalde et al. 2023; Roman Viminacium & Sirmium; Apollonia; Illyrian/Thracian onomastics in epigraphy","genetics":"Genetic continuity from Iron-Age Illyrians/Thracians; minimal Italic input; a distinct imperial-era influx of Anatolian-related ancestry integrated locally.","certainty":"documented"},{"era":"Medieval Balkans \u2014 Slavic settlement over a late-Roman substrate","start":500,"end":1000,"title":"When the Slavs Came, the Old Blood Stayed","blurb":"After Rome's Danube frontier collapsed, Slavic-speaking newcomers moved into the Balkans from the 6th century on \u2014 but they did not replace the people already there; they mixed into them. Ancient genomes from Croatia and Serbia (Olalde et al. 2023) show this incoming northern/eastern-European-like ancestry settling in as roughly 30-60% of today's Balkan gene pool, layered over a deep local substrate. In the rugged northwest and the Albanian highlands the older Illyrian-descended population absorbed the least Slavic admixture, which is why this corner still carries the freshest, least-diluted thread of the original Anatolian-farmer signal.","sites":"Olalde et al. 2023 (Cell) \u2014 146 genomes from Serbia & Croatia; Viminacium; the Dalmatian/Illyrian coastal zone","genetics":"Modern Balkan = late-Roman Balkan substrate + ~30-60% early-Slavic admixture; Albanians retained the most pre-Slavic ancestry (later Slavic input only ~10-20%); Y-lineages E-V13 and J2b common.","certainty":"documented"}]},"shared":{"label":"The Roots We All Share","dnaPct":100,"cards":[{"era":"Pan-African origin of Homo sapiens (Jebel Irhoud)","start":-315000,"end":-200000,"title":"The African dawn of our kind","blurb":"The story ends where humanity begins: in Africa, roughly 300,000 years ago. The fossils from Jebel Irhoud in Morocco \u2014 modern-faced but with a longer, more archaic braincase, dated to about 315,000 years \u2014 pushed the emergence of Homo sapiens back further than once thought and pointed to a pan-African process rather than a single birthplace. Toolmaking, fire, pigment and the gradual assembly of the modern face and mind all belong to this deepest layer. Every person in this lineage, and every human alive, traces back to these African ancestors.","sites":"Jebel Irhoud (Morocco, ~315 ka); Florisbad (South Africa); Omo Kibish & Herto (Ethiopia)","genetics":"Deep, structured pan-African ancestry; the common ancestral pool of all living humans; root of every present-day mitochondrial and Y-chromosome lineage","certainty":"well-supported"},{"era":"The Out-of-Africa founder dispersal","start":-52000,"end":-45000,"title":"The journey that peopled the world","blurb":"The single major dispersal of modern humans that founded every living non-African population is now dated by ancient DNA to roughly 50,000-47,000 years ago \u2014 the common doorway through which this whole lineage walked. (Earlier sapiens reached the Levant well before this, around 130,000-90,000 years ago at Skhul and Qafzeh, but those groups left little or no ancestry in people today.) As the founder population moved into Eurasia it met and mixed with Neanderthals \u2014 and in Asia, Denisovans \u2014 picking up the archaic DNA we all still carry. Every branch above narrows here, to one small band of travelers.","sites":"Skhul & Qafzeh and Misliya (Levant, earlier non-founder sapiens); Bacho Kiro & Ranis (early Eurasian modern humans); genetic founder modeling of all non-Africans","genetics":"All non-Africans descend from this dispersal; severe founder bottleneck; ~2% Neanderthal ancestry acquired in a shared admixture event ~47,000 years ago; deepest shared non-African node","certainty":"well-supported"},{"era":"Aurignacian \u2014 first modern Europeans","start":-43000,"end":-33000,"title":"Cro-Magnon arrives, and the caves come alive","blurb":"Some of the first widespread anatomically modern humans in Europe brought a creative surge: blade tools, bone flutes, ivory beads, the lion-headed figurine, and the earliest painted caves. They shared the cold landscape with the last Neanderthals; the small percentage of Neanderthal DNA everyone in this lineage carries comes mainly from a single, earlier admixture event in the Near East (~47,000 years ago), not from this period. These Aurignacian pioneers are a founding modern layer in Europe, though several of the very earliest individuals (such as Oase) belong to side-branches that left little ancestry in later Europeans.","sites":"Hohle Fels (the oldest Venus figurine and a bone flute); Hohlenstein-Stadel (the Lion-man / Lowenmensch); Chauvet & later Lascaux; Pestera cu Oase (Romania, individual with a recent Neanderthal ancestor)","genetics":"Among the earliest modern-human Europeans; carry ~2% Neanderthal admixture from an earlier shared event; a deep founding layer, with some early individuals not directly ancestral to later WHG","certainty":"well-supported"},{"era":"Gravettian Europe (Vestonice cluster)","start":-33000,"end":-22000,"title":"Mammoth hunters and the first Venus figurines","blurb":"Across Ice Age Europe a culturally connected people hunted mammoth, built shelters partly from their bones, fired the world's first known ceramic figurines, twisted plant fibers into cordage, and carved the 'Venus' statuettes that are among humanity's oldest portable art. Genetically the Vestonice cluster is an earlier European layer, largely replaced after the Last Glacial Maximum by the Villabruna ancestry that defines the Western Hunter-Gatherers \u2014 but they belong to the deep early-modern-human substrate of the continent and shared symbolic ideas across thousands of miles.","sites":"Dolni Vestonice & Pavlov (Czechia); Sungir (Russia); Willendorf, Gravette and Lespugue Venus figurines","genetics":"Vestonice cluster \u2014 a pre-LGM European hunter-gatherer lineage, largely superseded by Villabruna/WHG ancestry after the glacial maximum","certainty":"reconstructed"},{"era":"Ancient North Eurasians (Mal'ta-Buret')","start":-22000,"end":-15000,"title":"The ghost lineage of the frozen north","blurb":"Deep in Ice Age Siberia, a small population of mammoth-steppe hunters left a buried child at Mal'ta near Lake Baikal \u2014 the boy MA-1, whose ~24,000-year-old genome reshaped our picture of the human family tree. The Ancient North Eurasians sit genetically between western Eurasians and the ancestors of Native Americans: one strand of their ancestry flowed toward the first Americans, another fed into European foragers and, later, the steppe herders. They are a faint but real thread running through nearly everyone in this lineage \u2014 a population known almost entirely from DNA.","sites":"Mal'ta & Buret' (Lake Baikal, Siberia); Afontova Gora (Yenisei); MA-1 genome","genetics":"ANE \u2014 intermediate between West Eurasians and Native Americans; contributed to WHG, to EHG/steppe ancestry, and ~30% of First-American ancestry; MA-1 carries basal Y-haplogroup R*","certainty":"reconstructed"},{"era":"Western Hunter-Gatherers (Villabruna / Oberkassel cluster)","start":-15000,"end":-8000,"title":"Cheddar Man's people \u2014 dark skin, light eyes","blurb":"As the ice sheets retreated, a relatively uniform population of foragers recolonized Europe from the British Isles to the Carpathians, hunting red deer and aurochs, fishing the rivers, and leaving their dead in caves. Ancient DNA suggests most carried dark-to-intermediate skin alleles inherited from out-of-Africa ancestors together with frequently light (blue or blue-green) eyes \u2014 though newer studies (2024-2025) find more pigmentation variation than first reported and caution that these reconstructions are probabilistic. Britain's Cheddar Man (c. 8500-8200 BCE) is the best-known individual. They are the indigenous European substrate \u2014 the deepest of the three ancestral streams \u2014 partly absorbed when farmers arrived.","sites":"Cheddar Gorge (Cheddar Man); Villabruna & Arene Candide (Italy); Oberkassel (Germany); Loschbour (Luxembourg); La Brana (Spain)","genetics":"WHG = Villabruna/Oberkassel cluster (largely Villabruna ancestry with a minor older Magdalenian/Goyet-Q2 component); dark-to-intermediate skin alleles plus a high frequency of light-eye variants; probabilistic phenotype predictions","certainty":"well-supported"},{"era":"Anatolian Early Farmers + the Tas Tepeler world","start":-9500,"end":-7000,"title":"Monuments before farming \u2014 the Stone Hills","blurb":"In the limestone hills of southeastern Anatolia, late foragers and the earliest farmers raised some of the oldest monumental architecture on Earth: rings of T-shaped pillars carved with foxes, vultures, scorpions and snakes. Gobekli Tepe and its sister sites in the Tas Tepeler ('Stone Hills') program \u2014 Karahan Tepe foremost \u2014 complicate the old story that farming came first and monuments followed. Recent excavations show these were not bare sanctuaries but settlements with houses, hearths and burials, and the 2025 season at Karahan Tepe yielded the first human face carved on a T-shaped pillar, alongside statues such as the seated 'ribbed man.' From this cultural world the first farmers carried seeds, herds, and the Anatolian component of European ancestry into Europe.","sites":"Gobekli Tepe; Karahan Tepe (2025 face-carved T-pillar, seated 'ribbed man' statue); Sayburc; Boncuklu Tarla; wider Tas Tepeler / Sanliurfa program","genetics":"Anatolian Neolithic / Early European Farmer (EEF) ancestry \u2014 the source population that carried farming into Europe from ~7000-6500 BCE, mixing with and largely absorbing local foragers","certainty":"well-supported"},{"era":"Caucasus-Lower Volga (CLV) cline \u2014 Proto-Indo-Anatolians","start":-4500,"end":-3500,"title":"The cradle of a language family","blurb":"Resolved by ancient DNA in 2025 (Lazaridis et al., Nature), the Caucasus-Lower Volga people are the current best candidate for the Indo-Anatolian homeland: a genetic cline running from Neolithic Caucasus communities in the south to herders on the lower Volga in the north. They appear to be the parent population that gave rise both to the Anatolian-speaking branch (Hittites and kin) and, for about four-fifths of its ancestry, to the Yamnaya. The roughly 400-plus Indo-European languages spoken today by more than 40 percent of the world's people trace back through this Eneolithic population.","sites":"Berezhnovka (lower Volga); Remontnoye; Caucasus Neolithic sites; Lazaridis et al. 2025 (Nature) genome panels","genetics":"~45-50% Caucasus Neolithic + ~30-35% Eastern Hunter-Gatherer (EHG) + a further component including Siberian-related ancestry; ancestral to both the Anatolian branch of IE and the Yamnaya","certainty":"reconstructed"},{"era":"Yamnaya / Western Steppe Herders","start":-3300,"end":-2600,"title":"Wheels, herds, dairy and the steppe blood","blurb":"On the Pontic-Caspian grasslands a mobile pastoralist people built their world on cattle, sheep, wagons and (increasingly) the horse, burying their dead under kurgan mounds reddened with ochre. From roughly 3000 BCE they expanded outward, carrying Indo-European language and a wave of male-biased ancestry across Europe within a few centuries. They passed down the dominant European Y-chromosome lineages (R1b and R1a) and a large share of many Europeans' DNA; lactase persistence rose to high frequency in their descendants later, in the Bronze and Iron Ages, rather than being fixed in the Yamnaya themselves. Ancient DNA published in Nature in 2025 shows the Yamnaya drew about four-fifths of their ancestry from an older Caucasus-Lower Volga population.","sites":"Yamnaya kurgans, Pontic-Caspian steppe; Khvalynsk; Berezhnovka (lower Volga); Remontnoye","genetics":"~80% Caucasus-Lower Volga (CLV) ancestry plus Eastern Hunter-Gatherer and a minor Anatolian-Farmer component; carriers of steppe Y-haplogroups R1b-M269 and R1a; later descendants spread lactase persistence","certainty":"well-supported"},{"era":"The Three-Ancestry Synthesis (Bronze Age Europe)","start":-2500,"end":-1500,"title":"Where the three streams became one people","blurb":"By the mid-third millennium BCE the three great ancestral rivers of Europe had merged into the genetic mosaic carried by nearly every living European: indigenous hunter-gatherers, Anatolian farmers, and steppe pastoralists braided into single communities. In any Bronze Age village you would have found the blended blood of all three, the proportions shifting by region but the recipe shared. This is the convergence point: trace any modern European bloodline back far enough and the lines stop branching and start merging here, into the same three sources.","sites":"Bell Beaker burials across Europe; Corded Ware cemeteries; Unetice culture (Central Europe); Reich-lab / Allen Ancient DNA Resource population panels","genetics":"Modern Europeans modeled as a 3-way mixture: Western Hunter-Gatherer (WHG), Anatolian/Early European Farmer (EEF), and Yamnaya-derived Steppe ancestry, in region-varying proportions","certainty":"well-supported"}]}},"lineGroup":{"maxfield":"nordic","mccollum":"nordic","thury":"nordic","harris":"british","garrison":"british","davis":"british","mailloux":"germanic","hepburn":"germanic"}};
